WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.000
- Welcome everyone. It's another Saturday.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.150
This means another coffee
break here on my channel

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:07.300
Firm Learning.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:10.070
My name is Heinrich and
today I want to talk about

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:13.720
a piece of advice that I
got from one of my mentors

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:15.240
when I worked at McKinsey.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.980
And it was one of the pieces of advice

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:19.590
that were most influential,

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:23.130
the most impactful to my
career as a consultant

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.460
when I spent six years at McKinsey.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:28.411
And even as of today, when I work today

00:00:28.411 --> 00:00:30.950
in the PropTech company in Munich

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:33.430
in the position of a
chief revenue officer,

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:36.920
I still think about that and
it's still important to me.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.520
So I want to dig into this with you today.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:42.700
I will keep this video a
bit more conversational,

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.000
a bit less edited, so let
me know in the comments

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.100
whether you like this
new or different style.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.380
Would be very interested
to hear your feedback.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.870
And here this comes really
from the perspective

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.440
of a young consultant,

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.300
someone who starts out at a
firm like McKinsey, BCG, Bain,

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.360
or any of these other
management consulting firms.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.820
And what this mentor of mine
told me is that there are

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.450
three stages that you progress through

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.900
that you can develop yourself
into as a consultant.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.850
And most people start at this first stage,

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.170
but people will very quickly find out

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:24.270
that in this first stage, you
will just not be successful

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:25.370
as a consultant.

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:27.590
So then you, at some point, you graduate

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:32.590
into this second stage and then
later into this third stage.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.460
So let's look into these stages.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.100
What do people do and why
are certain of these things

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.720
not really helpful, not really good?

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:45.100
Will really limit your career
if you're not realizing this

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.000
and if you're not able to at some point

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.900
move up these stages.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:53.230
The first stage is the stage
of a data gatherer, right?

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:56.600
So this is the mindset that
many young consultants have,

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.320
that maybe you got a task
from your project leader,

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.790
from your partner, to go out there

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:04.370
and conduct certain analysis.

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:06.130
And now, you know,
therefore these analysis

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:08.010
for this piece of work
that you need to do,

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:11.030
you maybe need certain
data or certain information

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.650
and you know that there
are these specific clients

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.410
that would have this information.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:19.300
So you go out there, you sit
together with these people,

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:22.410
and you want to do your best
to collect exactly this data

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:24.630
that you are looking
for to do the analysis,

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.410
to do the work that you need to do.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:29.040
So you are a data gatherer.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.760
You are treating your
clients as a source of data,

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.360
a source of information that you just need

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:36.640
to sit together with,

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.210
and then hopefully you will
get all the information out.

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:42.370
Of course, what is the problem with that?

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:45.450
The issue with that is
that these relationships

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.110
that you will build with clients,

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:50.300
if you follow this mindset,
this way of working,

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:53.690
these relationships are
highly transactional.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:56.690
You treat your counterpart,
your client counterpart,

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:00.560
not really as someone you're
interacting with at eye level,

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.730
but rather just as a source of data.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:08.170
And clearly this often does not
feel great for your clients.

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.220
It's not a great experience.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:12.710
And from your perspective,
it's just your job.

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:13.640
You're doing your job.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:17.330
You need this information
and you might even complain

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.940
or not understand if you're
not getting all the data

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:21.410
that you need from the client,

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:22.950
or you feel like he or she

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:24.670
is not sharing something with you.

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:26.940
You might go even to your project sponsor

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.050
and complain about the person and so on.

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:32.500
So these are behaviors of
these more junior consultants,

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.850
which clearly are not really helpful.

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:37.130
And I will now reflect when we talk about

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:41.200
the next two stages why this is the case.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.860
And one other key problem
here is that by this way,

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:47.870
you're really not using the opportunity

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:50.830
of getting more information, more insights

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:52.100
out of your client,

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:55.870
besides the very specific
things that you will ask them.

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:58.920
Because once a client realizes
that you are just treating

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:00.970
him or her as a data source,

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:04.210
often these people will just
only share exactly the things

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.530
that you asked them to share.

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:08.960
But clearly, often these
people know much more,

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:12.130
many more insights that
would be very helpful to you.

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:15.850
And this is then where the
second stage comes into play.

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:17.200
And this mentor of mine,

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.420
this other partner that
gave me this insight,

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:22.860
he called this stage the
stage of co-creation.

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:26.050
So often, usually around
like one year or so,

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:29.260
some people understand this
earlier, others more later,

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:33.270
at some point you will realize
that this way of working

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.090
is not really successful, right?

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:37.560
This is just not how it works.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:41.870
What you want to do instead
is to co-create solutions

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:43.010
with your clients, right?

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:45.660
So of course, at some point
you will ask your clients

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:48.200
also for information and for data,

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:50.170
but this is not the main purpose

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:52.060
of you interacting with them.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:54.760
Instead, you want to talk
to them and treat them

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.810
as someone at eye level
with whom you can co-create

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:00.830
the solutions with.

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:04.290
You don't want to fall into
the mindset that you collect

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:06.950
all the data, then you sit
alone in your team room

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:08.170
or at home, at home office.

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:11.760
And then you prepare some
analysis, crunch the numbers

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:14.800
for weeks, and then after several weeks,

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.940
you go back to the client and show him,

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:18.970
wow, this is what I created.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:20.470
Isn't that amazing?

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:23.930
Isn't that a great piece of
work that I accomplished here?

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:26.480
This is just often not what works well.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:29.630
Maybe it's even correct,
but if the clients feel like

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:33.290
this is not really something
where they had also opportunity

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:35.880
to partake in the whole ideation process,

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:39.660
if it's not really something
where they at least feel like

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:41.780
they also had some buy-in,

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:45.000
they also had some input
that they could give,

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.490
this often does not go away.

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:48.760
At the end of the day, the perfect idea,

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:51.680
the perfect solution,
is not really valuable

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:54.090
if the client organization
is not buying it.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:57.430
If in the end, the client
organization is not acting on it.

00:05:57.430 --> 00:05:59.260
So really think hard about how can you

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:02.530
co-create the solutions with these people?

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:04.980
Are there some great
effective workshop formats

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:07.090
you can have together with these people?

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:10.010
I even saw setups where
we shared our team room

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:13.240
in consulting with a
couple of our key clients.

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:16.290
We really work together
with them every single day,

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:19.050
got their input, involve them in all our

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.790
also internal problem solving meetings.

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:24.660
These are the situations
where often you really form

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:28.750
very deep bonds with your
clients over the months

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:30.410
that the project that goes on.

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:32.650
You will get ideally their full buy-in.

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:35.230
They will feel like
everything that you created

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:37.710
was also co-created by them.

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:40.730
And ideally it is not even you in the end

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:42.720
presenting the results of the project

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.380
to your project stakeholders,
to your project owners.

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:48.570
What I've seen often, really
it's extremely powerful,

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:52.070
if it's the clients that
you work together with,

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:55.500
if it's them that then
stand in front of the board

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.280
and present the results,

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:00.030
present the outcomes of the project.

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:03.010
And of course it is clear that
you as the consulting firm

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:05.490
will then also be part of the meeting,

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:07.940
that you show you also
contributed to that.

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:09.900
But also for them, for the clients,

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:13.400
this is a great opportunity
to show what you created,

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:15.140
to show that they were proud of that,

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:17.120
that they also contributed to that.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:21.220
It increases their visibility
in the organization.

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:23.650
And by the way, if you don't
know what visibility is

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:26.140
and why it's so important,
I have a rather old,

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:29.450
but I think still very
relevant video on the topic.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:31.210
I will link it somewhere above here,

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:34.410
but this often turns your
clients into the stars,

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:37.080
into the successful heroes of the project.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:39.960
And this surely is a
way better way of really

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:42.050
building relationships with the clients

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:45.320
as just treating them as a data source.

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:48.802
But this is not necessarily
the end of this evolution

00:07:48.802 --> 00:07:50.200
of a consultant.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:55.200
Ideally, at some point you
are able to reach stage three.

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:58.090
And this is usually only
where you achieve this

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:00.190
as a senior project lead.

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:03.130
And then of course,
ideally at a partner stage

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:05.370
where you turn into something,

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:08.780
which this mentor of mine
that gave me this insight,

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:12.190
which is called the trusted advisor.

00:08:12.190 --> 00:08:13.980
This is, if you want to say it this way,

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:17.593
the last and final evolution
stage of a consultant.

00:08:18.490 --> 00:08:22.560
As a trusted advisor,
your client seeks you out

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:25.010
not only for the very specific project

00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:26.310
that you are conducting,

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:28.750
but also for any other question

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:30.660
that is relevant and important,

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:33.200
that is top of mind for the client.

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:36.330
You really positioned yourself as a person

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:39.360
who can really add value to any of these

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:41.500
business discussions that you're having.

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:45.290
And often you have very great
and significant opportunities

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:46.550
as a consultant,

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:49.350
especially in the model
where you're working on-site

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:50.257
together with the client.

00:08:50.257 --> 00:08:53.240
You really sit together with
your clients every single day.

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:54.920
And even, at least as a project lead,

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:57.110
you will often have the
opportunity to talk to

00:08:57.110 --> 00:09:00.140
the senior project sponsors,
which might be board members,

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:02.180
or at least senior business unit heads

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:03.120
or some equivalence of that.

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:06.130
You will have opportunities
to sit with these people

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:08.700
once per week, twice per
week, and talk to them.

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:12.480
And very often, if you have
conversations with them,

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:15.130
they will share also other
issues that they're having,

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:17.520
other challenges that they are facing

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:21.410
beyond the strict scope of your project.

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:24.420
These are often opportunities
for you to pick that up,

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:27.100
to offer some insights,
go to them and say,

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:28.710
hey, I understand that
this is really something

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:30.410
that's bothering you.

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:32.010
Why don't we sit together next week

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:33.500
and I will compile some knowledge,

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:37.040
some insights from that
of our expert networks,

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:39.550
of our insights that we have at the firm.

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:42.450
Let me connect you to
another expert that we have

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:45.780
in our firm that works
specifically on the topic.

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:49.410
Let me reach out to this
person, see what I can do here.

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:52.040
Try to help these people left and right.

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:54.270
And ideally at some point,

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:56.530
whenever these people have a problem,

00:09:56.530 --> 00:09:57.820
they will think of you.

00:09:57.820 --> 00:10:01.040
They will treat you as
their trusted advisor.

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:03.390
They will invite you
to lunch or to dinner,

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:05.310
or maybe you invite them
to lunch and to dinner.

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:08.450
And you talk about these things
and you offer your insights,

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:10.150
offer your recommendations.

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:14.410
And this, of course, is
the basis of a successful

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:17.500
long-term career in professional services.

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:20.090
I would argue this is true,
not only for consulting,

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:23.250
but also for investment banking
and many other professional

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:25.020
services areas as well,

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:26.750
but you really create a relationship

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:28.090
with one of your clients,

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:31.870
which goes far beyond
just the strict boundaries

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:33.460
of a specific project.

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:36.230
So these are the three
stages that most consultants

00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:37.063
go through.

00:10:37.063 --> 00:10:39.260
So you start as a data gatherer,

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:42.170
you develop into a co-creator,
and then at some point,

00:10:42.170 --> 00:10:45.200
hopefully eventually you
become a trusted advisor

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:46.730
of some of your clients.

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:49.323
And of course, if you're now
starting out in consulting,

00:10:49.323 --> 00:10:52.670
ask yourself, what can
you do to really co-create

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:56.220
your solutions more with the
client to get their buy-in,

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:59.040
to align ideas and your
thoughts with them,

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:02.020
to really make them part of the process?

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:04.750
Do not just treat them as a data source.

00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:07.970
And frankly, being able
to do that, you showing

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:12.050
that you can do that is often
also one of the main things

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:14.410
that consulting firms look out for

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:17.730
around your first year or
so when the first decisions

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:21.680
are due to promote you to the
next stage, to the next level.

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:24.640
These are really the essential
skills that will make you

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:26.340
successful as a consultant.

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:29.020
And that will be relevant
in your evaluation processes

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:30.010
as well.

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:32.600
So as always, if you took
any value out of this video

